m2c/tests/runner/test_runner_flow.py -- Patch C24: propose -> decide -> ask -> execute -> approval -> result.

Real C05 tools, real C06 policy, real C07 shell, a fake link and a fake person (``ask``).  The protocol rules pinned
here (protocol/README.md sections 2, 3 and 5; fixtures 02 and 03):

* an allowed call is answered with ``approval{allow}`` and then a ``tool_result``;
* a denied call gets ``approval{deny}`` and NO ``tool_result``;
* the person's "allow for the session" is the EXACT call, in memory only;
* an edited call is re-checked, and what actually ran is the first thing the model reads.

m2c/tests/runner/test_runner_interact.py -- Patch L01a: ``ask_user`` and ``tasks`` through the tool runner.

Both tools run on the DEVICE and, like ``plan_exit``, have no ``run_tool`` executor: the runner handles them itself
after C06 has decided and after the argument checks every tool gets.  A fake screen (the ``question`` callable) and a
fake task sink stand in for the terminal UI that L01b writes.  Pinned here:

* a run PAUSES on a question and continues with the person's answer (an option, several, free text, both);
* a decline, a timeout, a cancel and a missing screen each end the call with a fixed message and never hang;
* ``tasks`` replaces the list through the sink, writes one LOCAL event per update and answers with counts;
* nothing the model wrote is echoed into a log, and the person's answer is redacted on its way out.

m2c/tests/runner/test_runner_ordering.py -- Patch C24: idempotency, replay, cancellation, concurrency, prompts.

The shape of the rules (protocol/README.md section 5, fixture 03):

* a ``call_id`` is executed at most once, however often it is delivered, and not at all when the server has already
  closed it (a later ``tool_finished`` in the replay, or in the local log after a restart);
* cancelling kills a running shell command; a call the server closed is not answered;
* reads run side by side, anything that changes something runs alone and in arrival order;
* the person is asked one question at a time.
